# Plots and charts made: lines, points and areas over two axes, bars side by
# side or stacked, upright or lying, histograms, pies and donuts, each with
# its axes' ticks computed (nice numbers, logarithmic scales, the calendar's
# steps for dates), a grid, a legend, a title and the axes' labels, its text
# set in a `font.Font` and its colours from a palette made in
# `image/colorspace`; written as SVG text or drawn into an `image` through
# `image/vector`, the same drawing either way.
#
# It draws a chart once, as a picture: no window, no zoom, no animation and
# no reading of a picture back into numbers. It reads no font file and finds
# no font by name: `font.parse` makes the font a caller gives, which must hold
# every character the chart's text holds. Text is set left to right without
# shaping, as `image/text` sets it. Writing the SVG or the image to a file is
# the program's: `write_file`, or `image/png` for the image.
#
# It fails with `invalid`, where a value, a size, a colour list or an axis is
# out of its range, a chart leaves its plot too little room for its text, or a
# logarithmic axis is asked to place a value at or below zero; `limit`, where
# an image would pass 16,384 pixels a side; and `not_found`, where the font
# lacks a character of the chart's text.
#
# Not written yet, each a `pending` block of its ledger: an area filled from a
# series' line to the left axis; lines and bands across a plot at a value;
# text and arrows placed on a plot, and a bar's value written on it; error
# bars about a series' points; box plots and violin plots of distributions;
# step, stair and stem plots, and the empirical distribution as steps; heat
# maps of a grid of values with a colour bar and colour maps; contour plots,
# of a grid and of a triangulation; polar plots; several plots in one figure,
# and a second axis on the right or the top; event plots and broken bars;
# arrows and streamlines of a vector field.

use chart/internal/bins as cutting
use chart/internal/build
use chart/internal/checks
use chart/internal/codes
use chart/internal/dates
use chart/internal/figure
use chart/internal/pie as pies
use chart/internal/samples
use chart/internal/scene
use chart/internal/ticks as steps
use font
use image
use math

## Codes

# The code of an input the function does not read: a value that is infinite,
# or not a number where a gap is not taken; a range that does not rise; a
# count of ticks or bins out of its range; a logarithmic base not above 1 or
# a value at or below zero on such an axis; a date outside the years 1 to
# 9999; a size, a text size or a palette out of range; a chart too small for
# its text. `ticks`, `nice`, `bin`, `palette` and `lay_out` answer it.
constant INVALID: str
    codes.INVALID

# The code of an image past 16,384 pixels a side, which `lay_out` answers.
constant LIMIT: str
    codes.LIMIT

## Axes and their ticks

# How an axis places its values.
variant Scale
    # In proportion: equal steps of value are equal steps on the axis.
    linear
    # In proportion to the values' logarithms: equal ratios are equal steps,
    # and only values above zero have a place.
    log
        # The base whose powers are ticked, above 1: 10, 2 and the number e
        # are the usual ones.
        base: f64
    # Instants as seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z, read in UTC and ticked
    # at the calendar's steps, from year 1 to 9999.
    time

# Two ends of a range of values.
record Range
    # The low end.
    low: f64
    # The high end, at or above `low`.
    high: f64

# A tick of an axis.
record Tick
    # Where it stands, in the axis's values: seconds since 1970 on a time
    # axis.
    value: f64
    # Its label, empty where the tick is drawn without one, as the multiples
    # between two powers of a crowded logarithmic axis are.
    label: str

# The ticks of an axis over `span`, about `count` of them where the scale
# can choose.
#
# - `span`: the values the axis spans; its ends finite, `low` at or below
#   `high`, both above zero on a logarithmic axis and within the years 1 to
#   9999 on a time axis.
# - `scale`: how the axis places values.
# - `count`: about how many ticks, 1 to 1,000; a step of 1, 2 or 5 times a
#   power of ten is taken, so the number found is near it, not it.
#
# On a linear axis the ticks are the multiples of that step within the range,
# as d3-array's `ticks` finds them, labelled with as many digits after the
# point as the step needs and the whole part grouped by threes with commas, as
# d3-format's `,f` writes them; a range of one value has one tick. On a
# logarithmic axis they are the powers of the base and, where the range holds
# fewer powers than `count`, their whole multiples, as d3-scale's `log.ticks`
# places them; a multiple is unlabelled where d3-scale's `log.tickFormat`
# leaves it so, and a label is the value's shortest decimal from 0.0001 to
# below a million and its exponent form, `1e6`, past either. On a time axis
# they fall on a step of seconds, minutes, hours, days, Sundays, months or
# years chosen as d3-time's `utcTicks` chooses it, and are labelled as
# d3-scale's `scaleUtc` labels them, in English: `:30`, `03:45`, `03 PM`,
# `Mon 05`, `Jan 05`, `January`, `2026`. The ticks rise.
#
# Fails `invalid` where the range, the scale or the count is out of its
# range. See also `nice`.
function ticks(span: Range, scale: Scale, count: i64) -> [Tick]?
    inner: figure.Scale = match scale
        .linear => .linear
        .log l  => .log(base: l.base)
        .time   => .time

    checks.count(count)?
    checks.span(low: span.low, high: span.high, s: inner)?
    n = to_f64(count)
    out: [Tick] @= []

    match scale
        .linear =>
            places @= 0

            if span.low == span.high
                out @ out.push(Tick(value: span.low, label: steps.short_label(span.low)))
                return ok(out)
            places @ steps.decimals(steps.step_of(start: span.low, stop: span.high, count: n))

            for t in steps.ticks(start: span.low, stop: span.high, count: n)
                out @ out.push(Tick(value: t, label: steps.label(x: t, places: places)))
        .log l =>
            ts = steps.log_ticks(low: span.low, high: span.high, base: l.base, count: n)
            shown = steps.log_labelled(values: ts, base: l.base, count: n)

            for i in range(from: 0, to: ts.len())
                if shown[i]
                    out @ out.push(Tick(value: ts[i], label: steps.short_label(ts[i])))
                else
                    out @ out.push(Tick(value: ts[i], label: ""))
        .time =>
            if span.low == span.high
                t = to_i64(math.floor(span.low * 1000.0)).must()
                out @ out.push(Tick(value: span.low, label: dates.label(t)))
            else
                interval = dates.choose(start: span.low * 1000.0, stop: span.high * 1000.0, count: n)

                for t in dates.ticks_of(start: span.low * 1000.0, stop: span.high * 1000.0, interval: interval)
                    out @ out.push(Tick(value: to_f64(t) / 1000.0, label: dates.label(t)))

    return ok(out)

# `span` moved out to the ticks of about `count`, so that the axis begins
# and ends on a tick.
#
# - `span`: the values the axis must hold, as `ticks` takes them.
# - `scale`: how the axis places values.
# - `count`: about how many ticks, 1 to 1,000.
#
# A linear range moves out to the step of its ticks as d3-scale's linear
# `nice` moves it, at most ten rounds; a logarithmic one to whole powers of
# its base; a time range to the ticks of the step d3-time would choose. A
# range of one value is given back as it is. Fails `invalid` as `ticks` does.
# See also `ticks`.
function nice(span: Range, scale: Scale, count: i64) -> Range?
    inner: figure.Scale = match scale
        .linear => .linear
        .log l  => .log(base: l.base)
        .time   => .time

    checks.count(count)?
    checks.span(low: span.low, high: span.high, s: inner)?

    if span.low == span.high
        return ok(span)
    n = to_f64(count)

    niced = match scale
        .linear => steps.nice(low: span.low, high: span.high, count: n)
        .log l  => steps.log_nice(low: span.low, high: span.high, base: l.base)
        .time =>
            ms = dates.nice(start: span.low * 1000.0, stop: span.high * 1000.0, count: n)
            steps.Span(low: ms.low / 1000.0, high: ms.high / 1000.0)

    return ok(Range(low: niced.low, high: niced.high))

## Histograms' bins

# How a histogram's values are cut into bins.
variant Bins
    # Sturges' count of bins (one more than the base-2 logarithm of the count
    # of values, rounded up) with edges at the nice ticks of the values'
    # range, as d3-array's `bin` makes them by default.
    auto
    # A count of bins of one width from the least value to the greatest, as
    # NumPy's `histogram` makes them.
    count
        # How many, 1 to 10,000.
        count: i64
    # The bins between edges the program gives.
    edges
        # The edges, at least two, finite and rising strictly; a value outside
        # the first and the last is not counted.
        edges: [f64]

# A bin of a histogram: the values from `low` up to `high`; the last bin also
# takes a value equal to its `high`.
record Bin
    # The low edge.
    low: f64
    # The high edge, above `low`.
    high: f64
    # How many values fell in it.
    count: i64

# `values` counted into bins.
#
# - `values`: the values; one that is not a number is not counted.
# - `bins`: how the bins are cut.
#
# The bins rise and touch, each a value's from its low edge up to its high
# one. Where every value is the same, `auto` and `count` make one bin from
# half below it to half above, as NumPy widens such a range; with no value,
# `auto` makes no bin and `count` cuts the range 0 to 1, as NumPy does.
# Fails `invalid` where a value is infinite, a count is out of its range or
# the edges are fewer than two, not finite or not rising. See also `lay_out`.
function bin(values: [f64], bins: Bins) -> [Bin]?
    for i in range(from: 0, to: values.len())
        v = values[i]

        if v == v && !math.is_finite(v)
            return fail(code: codes.INVALID, msg: f"the value {v} at {i} is infinite, which no bin holds")

    made: [cutting.Bin] @= []

    match bins
        .auto => made @ cutting.automatic(values)
        .count c =>
            if c.count < 1 || c.count > 10000
                return fail(code: codes.INVALID, msg: f"the count of bins is 1 to 10000, not {c.count}")
            made @ cutting.uniform(values: values, count: c.count)
        .edges e =>
            if e.edges.len() < 2
                return fail(code: codes.INVALID, msg: f"the bins need two edges or more, not {e.edges.len()}")

            for i in range(from: 0, to: e.edges.len())
                if !math.is_finite(e.edges[i])
                    return fail(code: codes.INVALID, msg: f"the edge {e.edges[i]} at {i} is not a finite number")

                if i > 0 && e.edges[i] <= e.edges[i - 1]
                    return fail(
                        code: codes.INVALID,
                        msg: f"the edge {e.edges[i]} at {i} does not rise above the one before it"
                    )

            made @ cutting.between(values: values, edges: e.edges)

    out: [Bin] @= []

    for b in made
        out @ out.push(Bin(low: b.low, high: b.high, count: b.count))

    return ok(out)

## Colours

# `count` colours for the series of a chart, of hues evenly spaced around
# the circle from a blue, as light and as saturated as one another in HLS,
# taken in an order that sets each far from the one before it.
#
# - `count`: how many, 1 to 360.
#
# Fails `invalid` where the count is out of its range. See also `look`.
function palette(count: i64) -> [image.Color]?
    if count < 1 || count > 360
        return fail(code: codes.INVALID, msg: f"a palette holds 1 to 360 colours, not {count}")
    return ok(build.hues(count))

## Charts

# The frame of a chart: its size, its title and how its text and colours
# look. Built directly, or by `look`.
record Look
    # The width in pixels, 1 to 16,384.
    width: i64
    # The height in pixels, 1 to 16,384.
    height: i64
    # The title over the plot, or empty for none.
    title: str
    # The size of the tick labels', axis labels' and legend's em in pixels,
    # above 0; the title's is 1.4 times as large.
    text_size: f64
    # The ground.
    background: image.Color
    # The text, the axis lines and the steps.
    ink: image.Color
    # The grid's lines.
    grid: image.Color
    # The series' colours, at least one, taken in turn and again from the
    # first once each has been taken.
    palette: [image.Color]
    # Whether the named series are listed beside the plot.
    legend: bool

# The look of an ordinary chart: text 12 pixels to the em in a near black on
# white, a light gray grid, ten colours of `palette` and a legend.
#
# - `title`: the title, or empty for none.
# - `width`: the width in pixels.
# - `height`: the height in pixels.
#
# See also `palette`.
function look(title: str, width: i64, height: i64) -> Look
    return Look(
        width: width,
        height: height,
        title: title,
        text_size: 12.0,
        background: image.Color(r: 255, g: 255, b: 255, a: 255),
        ink: image.Color(r: 34, g: 34, b: 34, a: 255),
        grid: image.Color(r: 224, g: 224, b: 224, a: 255),
        palette: build.hues(10),
        legend: true
    )

# What range an axis spans.
variant Extent
    # The data's, moved out to the steps.
    data
    # This range, as given: what falls outside it is cut.
    fixed
        # The low end.
        low: f64
        # The high end, above `low`.
        high: f64

# How many ticks an axis takes.
variant TickCount
    # As many as its length holds: one each 6.5 text sizes along, one each
    # 3.5 up, two at least.
    auto
    # About this many, 1 to 1,000, as `ticks` takes it.
    about
        # The count.
        count: i64

# An axis of a chart. Built directly, or by `axis`.
record Axis
    # The axis's label, or empty for none.
    label: str
    # How it places its values.
    scale: Scale
    # The range it spans.
    extent: Extent
    # How many ticks it takes.
    ticks: TickCount
    # Whether a line of the grid crosses the plot at each tick.
    grid: bool

# A linear axis over the data's range, its ticks as many as its length holds
# and its grid drawn.
#
# - `label`: the axis's label, or empty for none.
#
# See also `look`.
function axis(label: str) -> Axis
    return Axis(label: label, scale: .linear, extent: .data, ticks: .auto, grid: true)

# How a series is drawn.
variant Mark
    # A line through its points in their order, broken where a value is not a
    # number.
    line
    # A disc at each point.
    points
    # A line and a disc at each point.
    line_and_points
    # A line, and the room between it and zero filled in the series' colour
    # at two fifths of its strength; stacked, the room between it and the
    # area below, filled whole.
    area

# A series of points.
record Series
    # The name the legend shows, or empty to leave it out of the legend.
    name: str
    # The columns, in the x axis's values; as many as `ys`.
    xs: [f64]
    # The rows, in the y axis's values.
    ys: [f64]
    # How it is drawn.
    mark: Mark

# Series over two axes. Built directly.
record Plot
    # The frame.
    look: Look
    # The axis along the bottom.
    x: Axis
    # The axis up the left side.
    y: Axis
    # The series, drawn in their order, each in the palette's colour of its
    # place.
    series: [Series]
    # Whether the `area` series stack, each on the ones before it; they then
    # share their columns.
    stacked: bool

# How bars of several series share a category.
variant Grouping
    # Side by side.
    grouped
    # One on another, the positive values up from zero and the negative down.
    stacked

# Which way bars run.
variant Direction
    # Up from the categories along the bottom.
    vertical
    # Right from the categories down the left side, the first at the top.
    horizontal

# The values of one series of bars.
record BarSeries
    # The name the legend shows, or empty to leave it out of the legend.
    name: str
    # One value a category, in the categories' order; one that is not a
    # number makes no bar.
    values: [f64]

# Bars of categories. Built directly.
record Bars
    # The frame.
    look: Look
    # The categories' names, at least one.
    categories: [str]
    # The label of the categories' axis, or empty for none.
    category_label: str
    # The axis of the values, which spans zero unless its extent is fixed.
    value: Axis
    # The series, each in the palette's colour of its place.
    series: [BarSeries]
    # How the series share a category.
    grouping: Grouping
    # Which way the bars run.
    direction: Direction

# The counts of values in bins. Built directly.
record Histogram
    # The frame; the bars take the palette's first colour.
    look: Look
    # The values counted.
    values: [f64]
    # How they are cut into bins.
    bins: Bins
    # The axis of the values.
    x: Axis
    # The axis of the counts.
    y: Axis

# A slice of a pie.
record Slice
    # The name the legend shows, or empty to leave it out of the legend.
    name: str
    # Its value, 0 or more; its share of the pie is its part of the sum.
    value: f64

# Shares of a whole. Built directly.
record Pie
    # The frame.
    look: Look
    # The slices, clockwise from twelve o'clock, each in the palette's colour
    # of its place.
    slices: [Slice]
    # The hole's radius as a share of the pie's, 0 for a pie and below 1 for a
    # donut.
    hole: f64
    # Whether each slice's share is written on it, where the text fits inside
    # the slice.
    percentages: bool

# A chart.
variant Chart
    # Series over two axes: lines, points and areas.
    plot
        # The plot.
        plot: Plot
    # Bars of categories.
    bars
        # The bars.
        bars: Bars
    # A histogram.
    histogram
        # The histogram.
        histogram: Histogram
    # A pie or a donut.
    pie
        # The pie.
        pie: Pie

## Drawn

# A chart laid out, ready to be written as SVG and drawn as an image, the
# same drawing either way. Made by `lay_out`.
record Layout
    # What is drawn, in pixels, in its order.
    scene: scene.Scene
    # The font its text is set in.
    font: font.Font

# How an SVG holds a chart's text.
variant SvgText
    # As text elements naming the font's family, then `sans-serif`: a reader
    # sets the text in the font it has of that name, so the text can be
    # selected and searched, and is placed as the font measured it.
    elements
    # As the outlines of the font's glyphs: every reader draws it alike, and
    # it is no longer text.
    outlines

# `c` laid out in `f`: its ranges and ticks found, its margins measured from
# its text, its shapes mapped to pixels and cut to the plot.
#
# - `c`: the chart.
# - `f`: the font its text is set in; it must hold every character of the
#   chart's title, labels, legend and steps.
#
# Fails `invalid` where the look's size is below 1, its text size is not a
# finite number above 0 and at most 1,000, its palette is empty, a value or an
# axis is out of its range (as `ticks` and `bin` say), a series' columns and
# rows differ in number, stacked areas differ in their columns, a pie's value
# is below zero or its values sum to zero, its hole is not from 0 up to 1, or
# the chart leaves its plot less than two text sizes a side; `limit` where a
# side passes 16,384 pixels; `not_found` where `f` lacks a character. See
# also `to_svg`, `to_image`.
function lay_out(c: Chart, f: font.Font) -> Layout?
    frame = match c
        .plot p      => p.plot.look
        .bars b      => b.bars.look
        .histogram h => h.histogram.look
        .pie p       => p.pie.look

    if frame.width < 1 || frame.height < 1
        return fail(code: codes.INVALID, msg: f"a chart is 1 pixel a side or more, not {frame.width} by {frame.height}")

    if frame.width > scene.MOST_PIXELS || frame.height > scene.MOST_PIXELS
        return fail(
            code: codes.LIMIT,
            msg: f"the chart would be {frame.width} by {frame.height} pixels, past {scene.MOST_PIXELS} a side"
        )

    if !math.is_finite(frame.text_size) || frame.text_size <= 0.0 || frame.text_size > 1000.0
        return fail(
            code: codes.INVALID,
            msg: f"the text size is a finite number above 0 and at most 1000, not {frame.text_size}"
        )

    if frame.palette.len() == 0
        return fail(code: codes.INVALID, msg: "the palette is empty")
    inner_look = figure.Look(
        width: frame.width,
        height: frame.height,
        title: frame.title,
        text_size: frame.text_size,
        background: frame.background,
        ink: frame.ink,
        grid: frame.grid,
        legend: frame.legend
    )

    given: [Axis] = match c
        .plot p      => [p.plot.x, p.plot.y]
        .bars b      => [b.bars.value]
        .histogram h => [h.histogram.x, h.histogram.y]
        .pie _       => []

    axes: [figure.Axis] @= []

    for a in given
        scale: figure.Scale = match a.scale
            .linear => .linear
            .log l  => .log(base: l.base)
            .time   => .time

        count = match a.ticks
            .auto    => 0
            .about n => n.count

        fixed = match a.extent
            .data    => Range(low: 0.0, high: 0.0)
            .fixed r => Range(low: r.low, high: r.high)

        inner = figure.Axis(
            label: a.label,
            scale: scale,
            fixed: a.extent != .data,
            low: fixed.low,
            high: fixed.high,
            count: count,
            grid: a.grid
        )
        checks.axis(inner)?
        axes @ axes.push(inner)

    made = match c
        .plot p =>
            series: [build.Series] @= []

            for s in p.plot.series
                mark: build.Mark = match s.mark
                    .line            => .line
                    .points          => .points
                    .line_and_points => .line_and_points
                    .area            => .area

                series @ series.push(build.Series(name: s.name, xs: s.xs, ys: s.ys, mark: mark))

            built = build.plot(series: series, palette: frame.palette, stacked: p.plot.stacked, size: frame.text_size, time_x: p.plot.x.scale == .time, time_y: p.plot.y.scale == .time)?
            figure.layout(look: inner_look, x: axes[0], y: axes[1], shapes: built.shapes, entries: built.entries, f: f)?
        .bars b =>
            if b.bars.categories.len() == 0
                return fail(code: codes.INVALID, msg: "the bars have no category")
            names: [str] @= []
            values: [[f64]] @= []

            for s in b.bars.series
                names @ names.push(s.name)
                values @ values.push(s.values)

            horizontal = b.bars.direction == .horizontal

            log = match b.bars.value.scale
                .log _          => true
                .linear | .time => false

            built = build.bars(categories: b.bars.categories.len(), names: names, values: values, palette: frame.palette, stacked: b.bars.grouping == .stacked, horizontal: horizontal, log: log)?
            band = figure.Axis(
                label: b.bars.category_label,
                scale: .band(names: b.bars.categories),
                fixed: false,
                low: 0.0,
                high: 0.0,
                count: 0,
                grid: false
            )

            if horizontal
                figure.layout(look: inner_look, x: axes[0], y: band, shapes: built.shapes, entries: built.entries, f: f)?
            else
                figure.layout(look: inner_look, x: band, y: axes[0], shapes: built.shapes, entries: built.entries, f: f)?
        .histogram h =>
            log_x = match h.histogram.x.scale
                .log _          => true
                .linear | .time => false

            for i in range(from: 0, to: h.histogram.values.len())
                v = h.histogram.values[i]

                if log_x && v == v && v <= 0.0
                    return fail(
                        code: codes.INVALID,
                        msg: f"the value {v} at {i} is at or below zero, where a logarithmic axis has no place"
                    )

            build.check_values(name: "", what: "the histogram", vs: h.histogram.values, gaps: true, time: h.histogram.x.scale == .time)?
            shapes: [figure.Shape] @= []

            for b in bin(values: h.histogram.values, bins: h.histogram.bins)?
                if b.count > 0
                    shapes @ shapes.push(
                        .box(x0: b.low, x1: b.high, y0: 0.0, y1: to_f64(b.count), color: frame.palette[0], gap_x: true, gap_y: false)
                    )

            figure.layout(look: inner_look, x: axes[0], y: axes[1], shapes: shapes, entries: [], f: f)?
        .pie p =>
            slices: [pies.Slice] @= []
            total @= 0.0

            for i in range(from: 0, to: p.pie.slices.len())
                s = p.pie.slices[i]

                if !math.is_finite(s.value) || s.value < 0.0
                    return fail(
                        code: codes.INVALID,
                        msg: f"the slice \"{s.name}\" has {s.value}; a slice's value is a finite number, 0 or more"
                    )
                total @ total + s.value
                slices @ slices.push(
                    pies.Slice(name: s.name, value: s.value, color: frame.palette[i % frame.palette.len()])
                )

            if !(total > 0.0) || !math.is_finite(total)
                return fail(
                    code: codes.INVALID,
                    msg: f"the slices' values sum to {total}; a pie needs a finite sum above zero"
                )

            if p.pie.hole != p.pie.hole || p.pie.hole < 0.0 || p.pie.hole >= 1.0
                return fail(code: codes.INVALID, msg: f"a pie's hole is 0 up to 1 of its radius, not {p.pie.hole}")
            pies.layout(look: inner_look, slices: slices, hole: p.pie.hole, percentages: p.pie.percentages, f: f)?

    return ok(Layout(scene: made, font: f))

# The SVG document of a laid out chart: one line ending with a line feed, no
# XML declaration, as wide and as high as its look in pixels.
#
# - `l`: the chart laid out.
# - `text`: how the text is held.
#
# Fails `not_found` where the font lacks a character, as `image/text` does.
# See also `to_image`.
function to_svg(l: Layout, text: SvgText) -> str?
    outlines = match text
        .elements => false
        .outlines => true

    return scene.svg(s: l.scene, f: l.font, outlines: outlines)

# A laid out chart drawn into an RGBA image as wide and as high as its look,
# anti-aliased, the text filled from the font's outlines.
#
# - `l`: the chart laid out.
#
# Fails `limit` where a side passes 16,384 pixels, and `not_found` where the
# font lacks a character. See also `to_svg`.
function to_image(l: Layout) -> image.Image?
    return scene.draw(s: l.scene, f: l.font)

## Examples

# The ticks of a linear axis from 0 to 1, at steps of a tenth.
test "ticks: a linear axis"
    ts = ticks(span: Range(low: 0.0, high: 1.0), scale: .linear, count: 5).must()
    assert ts.len() == 6
    assert ts[1].label == "0.2"

# A range moved out to whole tens.
test "nice: a range moved to its ticks"
    assert nice(span: Range(low: 3.0, high: 97.0), scale: .linear, count: 10).must() == Range(low: 0.0, high: 100.0)

# Eight values cut into Sturges' four bins at nice edges.
test "bin: the bins d3 makes"
    bs = bin(values: [1.0, 2.0, 2.0, 3.0, 3.0, 3.0, 4.0, 9.0], bins: .auto).must()
    assert bs[0] == Bin(low: 0.0, high: 2.0, count: 1)

# Three colours a third of the circle apart.
test "palette: three hues"
    assert palette(3).must().len() == 3

# An ordinary look, 640 by 400 pixels.
test "look: the ordinary frame"
    assert look(title: "Sales", width: 640, height: 400).text_size == 12.0

# A linear axis over the data.
test "axis: the ordinary axis"
    assert axis("Year").scale == .linear

# A line drawn and written as SVG.
test "lay_out: a line over two axes"
    f = font.parse(samples.truetype(), 0).must()
    p = Plot(
        look: look(title: "Rise", width: 320, height: 200),
        x: axis("x"),
        y: axis("y"),
        series: [Series(name: "a", xs: [0.0, 1.0, 2.0], ys: [1.0, 3.0, 2.0], mark: .line)],
        stacked: false
    )
    l = lay_out(.plot(plot: p), f).must()
    assert to_svg(l, .elements).must().slice(from: 0, to: 4) == "<svg"

# A pie written with its text as outlines.
test "to_svg: a pie's text as outlines"
    f = font.parse(samples.truetype(), 0).must()
    p = Pie(
        look: look(title: "", width: 200, height: 160),
        slices: [Slice(name: "a", value: 1.0), Slice(name: "b", value: 3.0)],
        hole: 0.0,
        percentages: true
    )
    svg = to_svg(lay_out(.pie(pie: p), f).must(), .outlines).must()
    assert svg.slice(from: svg.len() - 7, to: svg.len()) == "</svg>\n"

# Bars drawn into an image of the look's size.
test "to_image: bars as pixels"
    f = font.parse(samples.truetype(), 0).must()
    b = Bars(
        look: look(title: "", width: 240, height: 160),
        categories: ["a", "b"],
        category_label: "",
        value: axis(""),
        series: [BarSeries(name: "n", values: [2.0, 5.0])],
        grouping: .grouped,
        direction: .vertical
    )
    img = to_image(lay_out(.bars(bars: b), f).must()).must()
    assert img.width == 240
